      "writtenOn": "2026-11-02T16:31:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "needs_swiotlb_bounce() assumes any device with an IOMMU domain is mapped by dma-iommu, and for those devices it relies on SG_DMA_SWIOTLB. That is wrong:\n\n- Devices that don\u0027t use dma-iommu are mapped by dma-direct even if they do have an IOMMU domain, e.g. IOMMU_DOMAIN_IDENTITY via iommu.passthrough\u003d1, or a DMA domain whose dma-iommu setup failed. dma-direct never sets SG_DMA_SWIOTLB but can still bounce (swiotlb\u003dforce, restricted DMA pools, DMA mask limits). Such buffers are accepted, and the CPU and device see different data because uncached system heap buffers skip dma_sync_sgtable_for_*(), so mapping them must be refused if SWIOTLB bounced any segment, as only those calls copy data between the bounce buffer and the buffer.\n\n- dma-iommu sets SG_DMA_SWIOTLB on the table whenever it takes its SWIOTLB path, but only bounces segments that aren\u0027t aligned to the IOVA granule. Buffers whose segments are aligned are rejected even though nothing was bounced.\n\nInstead, check each DMA segment with swiotlb_find_pool(), as the SWIOTLB sync code does. Translate the DMA address through the IOMMU domain if use_dma_iommu(), and with dma_to_phys() otherwise.\n\nAs before, bouncing inside a device\u0027s own dma_map_ops is still not detected.",
      "revId": "7e1c157e87d3f4d0d745cb4b1f23673bad947d99",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
