      "writtenOn": "2026-11-02T16:31:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The kernel\u0027s own record of whether dma-iommu maps the device is the `DEV_FLAG_DMA_IOMMU`-flag which is exposed to drivers via `use_dma_iommu()`. It can differ from the domain type:\n\n1) `iommu_setup_dma_ops()` clears the flag if `iommu_dma_init_domain()` fails. The domain is then still DMA-type but dma-direct does the mapping, so later the code here will call `iommu_iova_to_phys()` on a physical address and miss a bounce.\n2) The vendor hook `trace_android_rvh_iommu_setup_dma_ops(dev)` runs after the flag is set. Pixel doesn\u0027t use it, but not sure about other vendors. The hook could change the dma ops. The hook could do anything, but let\u0027s ignore this case here.\n\n\nAlthough unlikely, #1 *could* happen; it can easily be addressed and I\u0027d suggest:\n\n```\n-\tstruct iommu_domain *domain \u003d iommu_get_domain_for_dev(dev);\n+\tstruct iommu_domain *domain \u003d NULL;\n \tstruct scatterlist *sg;\n \tint i;\n \n-\t/* Non-DMA-API domains (IDENTITY) are mapped by dma-direct, so treat them as no IOMMU. */\n-\tif (domain \u0026\u0026 !iommu_is_dma_domain(domain))\n-\t\tdomain \u003d NULL;\n+\t/*\n+\t * Only dma-iommu returns IOVAs, translate those through the IOMMU\n+\t * domain. Otherwise assume dma-direct, whose DMA addresses translate\n+\t * via dma_to_phys(). Bouncing done by a device\u0027s own dma_map_ops\n+\t * can\u0027t be detected here.\n+\t */\n+\tif (use_dma_iommu(dev))\n+\t\tdomain \u003d iommu_get_domain_for_dev(dev);\n```\n\n\nThe approach can still miss bounces:\n* dma-iommu device whose current domain is UNMANAGED (a driver attached its own domain) or NULL\n* dvice with its own dma_map_ops, no dma-iommu\n* CONFIG_IOMMU_DMA\u003dn\n\nbut I think those are out of scope judging by aosp/3042555.",
      "range": {
